t  acceleration more or less ought to be  it's probably a bit more complicated  because there will be local effects you  know that causing the sun to accelerate  there are nothing to do with that global  acceleration but actually it turns out  that the number they get here is almost  exactly the same as that figure you  would expect and then of course the last  bit is i mentioned that not only do you  get the size of the acceleration you get  the direction of the acceleration as  well  and the direction is pointed right at  the center of the galaxy so this is  exactly what you'd expect for that kind  of centripetal acceleration effect so  they've used all these stars and this  guy data to calculate something they  could have calculated pretty easily  another way you know at some level it's  a trivial calculation because we sort of  already knew what the answer is it's  sort of reassuring it gave the right  answer technically it's absolutely  amazing that they can measure an  acceleration directly measure you know  pre the previous you know we think the  acceleration has to be this was all  indirectly inferred right they've  measured it they've measured this  acceleration this tiny acceleration  you want it to point and keep it there  and it's actually surprisingly simple  physics and it's all down to the  conservation of angular momentum  now you might see in in some science  fiction shows like battlestar galactica  or something like that  that they maneuver spacecraft with  thrusters

